Z||000008||fierofrek||06-08-2001||11:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierokid87:<BR><B>Ok here we go.  Im doing the same conversion to my 87 coupe.  Basically just take the V-6 and the wiring hoses, ECM, and all the stuff that is different between the two cars.  when your done with that then it sould be simple.  I know I dont sound liek I know what Im talking about but trust me.  changing to a V-6 is simple.  So far the hardes part of the conversion is waiting on the engine to come back from the machine shop.   Hoping to have about 190 hp.<P>Scott</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>kewl, THX. hope you get to the 190+  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> wait, second question, do I need the dash cluster too? just thought about that.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fierokid87||06-09-2001||01:25 AM||||Yes it would help to have the dash cluster.  I have it in mine.  Remember though even though people might tell you you need to change the chassis wiring you dont.  you only have to swap out the engine wiring.  everything inside the car is the same.  Now in my case the coolant pipes, heater lins, and AC lines are the same accept the drive coolant pipe needs to be cut down alittle.  <P>Scott<P>Scott||||reg||10
Z||000010||turbo86se||06-09-2001||10:10 AM||||I just completed this conversion on my '88 coupe. My mechanic and I used a V6 from a '88 Formula. The wiring was too easy. I used a V6 auto tranny from a 85 SE V6 with a seized motor. The tranny got a new filter kit. Most of the hoses had to be extended or cut (vaccuum, heater, coolant...). <BR>Don't forget to replace your 4-cyl fuel pump with a V6 fuel pump. That V6 demands more fuel!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I didn't change the radiator, I've heard you're supposed to, mine cools it just fine. <BR>I used the guages from an '86SE with extra guages above vents. If you don't switch them out your tach will run 1000 RPM's higher. <BR>You'll have to change the detent/kickdown cable if it's an auto, and the throttle cable regardless. Do that while your interior is taken apart. <BR>It's not too much work, somehow it took my mechanic a year to do it, but I've put 2000 miles on it in a month, and the difference between a 4 and a 6 is night and day!<BR>HAVE FUN!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=1676320&p=20236791"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierofrek||06-09-2001||11:26 AM||||Thx for the replies. your making my job easier and easier!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> hadn't thought about the throttle or kickdown cables at all, that would have ticked me off to get it all set up, then have to take it back apart! hehe<BR>again Thx all <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
